findings.description:
The doc does not say what happens on:
- an exception raised inside the tick (NumPy error or engine exception): does the server crash, skip the tick, or restore?
- NaN or inf from integration (Euler, no clamp, as 100-arch's stability note warns);
- an environment load failure at startup;
- a log write failure (see the logging finding);
- a client disconnecting mid-send, handshake timeouts, or keepalive.

The project's failure-mode rule requires each I/O path to name its hang, timeout, and disconnect behavior, plus the observable signal (WARNING or higher, or a metric). Add a failure table per I/O path.

Step 6 says "sleep for the remainder of the tick budget, or log an overrun warning," and Technical Considerations says "warn on overrun, continue at best effort." Not specified:
- whether the next tick starts immediately (catch-up, which can make overruns self-sustaining) or the schedule resets (simulated time then falls behind wall time);
- whether the tick number, and therefore simulated time, advances by one per tick regardless of wall time. Slice 308 ("ticks map to simulated time") implies yes, but this doc does not say so;
- whether the wall-clock timestamp recorded in step 1 is an engine input. It must not be, or determinism breaks, and the doc does not say so.
